---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Bread Pitt: The Reboot"
  description="The story of rebuilding Bread Pitt 2.0 from a fresh jar of flour and water after the original starter was lost during the move from Florida to Texas."
>
  <article class="story-page wrap">
    <header class="story-header">
      <p class="eyebrow">Starter Story</p>
      <h1>Bread Pitt: The Reboot</h1>
      <p class="story-intro">
        The story of rebuilding a sourdough starter from scratch, including
        strange smells, slow progress, unexpected setbacks, and the return to
        the kitchen.
      </p>
    </header>

    <figure class="story-image story-image-feature">
      <img src="/images/starter-story/bread-pitt-1.jpg" alt="Bread Pitt Starter 1.0" />
      <figcaption>Bread Pitt 1.0. The original starter before the move from Florida to Texas.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <section class="story-section">
      <h2>The Inciting Incident</h2>
      <p>Bread Pitt 1.0 did not survive the move from Florida to Texas.</p>
      <p>
        I wish there were a more dramatic story behind it. I was moving and
        packing all my stuff into plastic totes, then putting them in a storage
        container and shipping it to Texas. There was no way Bread Pitt was
        going to survive the trip while spending two weeks in a 100-degree
        storage container.
      </p>
      <p>I had to put him down.</p>
      <p>Down the sink.</p>
      <p>Farewell, Bread Pitt 1.0.</p>
      <p>
        So on July 10, 2026, Bread Pitt 2.0 began his career with a clean jar,
        some flour, some water, and absolutely no acting experience.
      </p>
      <p>
        There are plenty of videos, tutorials, articles, schedules, charts, and
        strongly held opinions online about how to make a sourdough starter.
        Most of them will get you there.
      </p>
      <p>
        Your experience probably will not match theirs exactly, or mine for
        that matter.
      </p>
      <p>
        I have created several starters over the years, and every one of them
        has behaved a little differently. Some become active quickly. Some
        bubble for days without rising much. Some develop a pleasant sour smell
        early on. Others spend a few days smelling like something you found
        behind a gym locker.
      </p>
      <p>Bread Pitt chose the old-boot route.</p>
    </section>

    <figure class="story-image">
      <img src="/images/starter-story/starter-setup.jpg" alt="Bread Pitt Starter setup" />
      <figcaption>The beginning of Bread Pitt 2.0: flour, water, and a clean jar.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <section class="story-section">
      <h2>Assembling the Cast</h2>
      <p>The plan was simple:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Keep a small amount of starter.</li>
        <li>Feed it flour and water every 24 hours.</li>
        <li>Watch for bubbles, rise, texture changes, and strange smells.</li>
        <li>Try not to panic every time it did something weird.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>My regular feeding was:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>40 grams starter</li>
        <li>40 grams water</li>
        <li>40 grams flour</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        I kept Bread Pitt at around 74°F and marked the level on the jar after
        each feeding so I could see how much he rose.
      </p>
      <p>
        At the beginning, I used King Arthur unbleached bread flour. That was
        what I already had in the kitchen, and I did not feel like turning the
        first feeding into a supply run.
      </p>
      <p>
        Bread flour worked, although Bread Pitt developed slowly.
      </p>
      <p>
        I have used all-purpose flour for starters before and did not get great
        results. The flour was bleached, and I suspect that was part of the
        problem. Unbleached all-purpose flour can work, but after my previous
        experience, I felt better starting with bread flour.
      </p>
      <p>
        I would have preferred to use some whole-wheat flour from the beginning.
        I simply did not have any yet.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="story-section">
      <h2>The Seven-Day Prophecy</h2>
      <p>
        A lot of guides describe sourdough starter as a seven-day project.
      </p>
      <p>
        Day 1: Mix flour and water.<br />
        Day 3: See bubbles.<br />
        Day 5: Watch it double.<br />
        Day 7: Bake a beautiful loaf while sunlight streams through the kitchen
        window and everything looks suspiciously clean.
      </p>
      <p>
        One week is a useful general timeline. Some starters are ready that
        quickly. Others need two weeks. Some take longer.
      </p>
      <p>
        Bread Pitt took closer to three weeks before I trusted him with his
        first loaf.
      </p>
      <p>That was fine. He was working at his own pace.</p>
    </section>

    <figure class="story-image-grid">
      <img src="/images/starter-story/starter-early-01.jpg" alt="Bread Pitt Starter Early" />
      <img src="/images/starter-story/starter-early-02.jpg" alt="Bread Pitt Starter Early" />
      <img src="/images/starter-story/starter-early-03.jpg" alt="Bread Pitt Starter Early" />
    </figure>

    <section class="story-section">
      <h2>The Early Roles</h2>
      <p>
        The first couple of days were quiet. Bread Pitt looked like wet flour
        sitting at the bottom of a jar, which was a fair assessment of the
        situation.
      </p>
      <p>
        By Day 3, small bubbles had started appearing throughout the mixture.
        There was very little rise, but something was clearly happening.
      </p>
      <p>
        Bread Pitt had officially moved beyond “jar of paste” and landed his
        first supporting role.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="story-section">
      <h2>Something Smells Suspicious</h2>
      <p>
        Then came Day 4.
      </p>
      <p>
        The starter became loose and runny. It had more bubbles, almost no rise,
        and smelled like an old sweaty boot that had been living under the bed
        for a few weeks.
      </p>
      <p>
        The aroma suggested many things. Fresh bread was pretty far down the
        list.
      </p>
      <p>
        Young starters often pass through a strange-smelling phase while
        different bacteria compete inside the jar.
      </p>
      <p>
        Bread Pitt smelled like ripe footwear.
      </p>
      <p>
        His color still looked healthy. There was no mold, no pink or orange
        discoloration, and plenty of small bubbles. I kept feeding him.
      </p>
    </section>

    <figure class="story-image-grid">
      <img src="/images/starter-story/starter-smelly-01.jpg" alt="Bread Pitt Starter Smelly" />
      <img src="/images/starter-story/starter-smelly-02.jpg" alt="Bread Pitt Starter Smelly" />
    </figure>

    <section class="story-section">
      <h2>The Case of the Missing Feeding</h2>
      <p>
        On July 14, I forgot to feed Bread Pitt, and he was not happy about it.
      </p>
      <p>
        No sourdough emergency response team arrived.
      </p>
      <p>
        The next morning, he looked collapsed and hungry. I discarded the usual
        amount, gave him his regular feeding, and continued with the schedule.
      </p>
      <p>
        He was fine.
      </p>
      <p>
        Missing a feeding is not ideal, especially while a starter is
        developing, but one missed day did not ruin anything.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="story-section">
      <h2>The Long Second Act</h2>
      <p>
        Over the next several days, Bread Pitt kept producing bubbles. His smell
        gradually moved from old boot to neutral, then back to mild boot, then
        toward bread, yeast, and alcohol.
      </p>
      <p>
        The aroma kept improving while the rise stayed stubbornly small.
      </p>
      <p>
        By July 19, he had been alive for more than a week and still showed only
        a tiny amount of growth after feeding.
      </p>
      <p>
        It had risen approximately the width of a badly penciled-in eyebrow.
      </p>
      <p>
        I kept feeding him.
      </p>
    </section>

    <figure class="story-image-grid">
      <img src="/images/starter-story/starter-second-act-before-feed.jpg" alt="Bread Pitt before feeding" />
      <img src="/images/starter-story/starter-second-act-after-feed.jpg" alt="Bread Pitt after feeding" />
    </figure>

    <section class="story-section">
      <h2>The Whole-Wheat Side Quest</h2>
      <p>
        I wanted to add whole-wheat flour because it often gives a developing
        starter a useful boost.
      </p>
      <p>
        Buying it became a side quest.
      </p>
      <p>
        Somewhere nearby, a confused neighbor may have received five pounds of
        whole-wheat flour and a bag of limes while Bread Pitt continued eating
        bread flour.
      </p>
      <p>
        Once I finally got the whole wheat, I split the flour evenly:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>40 grams bread flour</li>
        <li>40 grams whole-wheat flour</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <figure class="story-image">
      <img src="/images/starter-story/starter-whole-wheat.jpg" alt="Bread Pitt Starter Whole Wheat" />
      <figcaption>Whole wheat joined the routine and Bread Pitt started gaining momentum.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <section class="story-section">
      <h2>The Training Montage</h2>
      <p>
        That was when his career started gaining momentum.
      </p>
      <p>
        The rise improved. The alcohol smell faded. The mixture held its
        structure better, and the activity became much easier to see.
      </p>
      <p>
        He began rising after each feeding, filling with small and medium
        bubbles, reaching a peak, and slowly falling again.
      </p>
    </section>

    <figure class="story-image-grid">
      <img src="/images/starter-story/starter-montage-01.jpg" alt="Bread Pitt Starter Montage" />
      <img src="/images/starter-story/starter-montage-02.jpg" alt="Bread Pitt Starter Montage" />
      <img src="/images/starter-story/starter-montage-03.jpg" alt="Bread Pitt Starter Montage" />
    </figure>

    <section class="story-section">
      <h2>The Dependable Climax</h2>
      <p>
        By July 28, Bread Pitt was regularly reaching around double his starting
        height.
      </p>
      <p>
        More importantly, he repeated that performance several days in a row.
      </p>
      <p>
        A single dramatic rise is exciting. Repeated, predictable rises are what
        made me trust him.
      </p>
      <p>
        I stopped wondering whether he was alive and started planning his first
        loaf.
      </p>
      <p>
        Bread Pitt had finally become dependable.
      </p>
    </section>

    <figure class="story-image loaf-feature">
      <img src="/images/starter-story/first-loaf.jpg" alt="Bread Pitt starter first loaf" />
      <figcaption>The first loaf made with Bread Pitt 2.0.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <section class="story-section">
      <h2>Lessons from the Credits</h2>
      <p>
        A sourdough starter uses simple ingredients and a simple routine: flour,
        water, time, and regular feeding. The process can still be messy, slow,
        smelly, and unpredictable.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Use unbleached flour.</li>
        <li>Bread flour worked well for me, and whole wheat gave Bread Pitt a noticeable boost.</li>
        <li>Use what you have.</li>
        <li>Treat one week as an estimate.</li>
        <li>Pay attention to patterns.</li>
        <li>Keep feeding through the strange phase.</li>
        <li>Missing one feeding is survivable.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Bread Pitt 2.0 took the scenic route to stardom, but he got there.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
